==English==
{{was wotd|2012|January|11}}
[[File:Ulmus americana (5107225627).jpg|upright=1.55|thumb|'''Petioles''' between leaf blades and stem.]]

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|pétiole}}, and its source, {{der|en|LL.|petiolus||little foot}}, diminutive form of {{der|en|la|pēs|t=foot}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpɛti.əʊl/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-petiole.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpɛti.oʊl/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|botany}} The [[stalk]] of a [[leaf]], attaching the [[blade]] to the [[stem]].
#: {{syn|en|pedicel}} {{gloss|stalk of a flower}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|author=Harry T. Valentine|title=Estimating Defoliation of Hardwoods Using Blade-'''petiole''' Relations|publisher={{w|US Forest Service|Forest Service}} Research Paper NE 405, {{w|US Department of Agriculture}}|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=lLscEtBxf70C&pg=PP3&dq=%22petiole%22%7C%22petioles%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjGt4byqN3pAhVhwTgGHZALDNoQ6AEIJzAA#v=onepage&q=%22petiole%22%7C%22petioles%22&f=false|page=1
|passage=Most insects consume tissue from the leaf blade were measured just past the twist on the side away only, leaving the leaf '''petioles''' unscathed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|author=Karl J. Niklas|title=Plant Biomechanics|publisher={{w|University of Chicago Press}}|url=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=l3bRJVMbNMcC&pg=PA167&dq=%22petiole%22%7C%22petioles%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjGt4byqN3pAhVhwTgGHZALDNoQ6AEIzQEwEw#v=onepage&q=%22petiole%22%7C%22petioles%22&f=false|page=167|text=By contrast, the '''petioles''' of large pinnate leaves, as well as stems, typically resist torsion by placing stiff materials with high elastic moduli (like sclerenchyma) toward the perimeters of their cross sections.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author=Mike Hansell|title=Bird Nests and Construction Behaviour|publisher={{w|Cambridge University Press}}|url=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=ySFS6HGDfLgC&pg=PA116&dq=%22petiole%22%7C%22petioles%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjGt4byqN3pAhVhwTgGHZALDNoQ6AEI8QUwVA#v=onepage&q=%22petiole%22%7C%22petioles%22&f=false|page=116|text=An example of this is leaf '''petioles'''. Some species of trees have pinnate leaves which, when the leaves fall, shed pinnae from the '''petiole''', which is then left as a tapering, somewhat flexible rod.}}
# {{lb|en|entomology|insect anatomy}} A narrow or constricted [[segment]] of the body of an [[insect]]; especially, the [[metasomal]] segment of certain [[Hymenoptera]], such as wasps.
#: {{syn|en|pedicel}} {{qualifier|used more generally, of arthropods}}
# {{lb|en|entomology}} The stalk at the base of the nest of the [[paper wasp]].

====Usage notes====
* The presence of a petiole (narrow body segment) is the defining characteristic distinguishing the suborder [[Apocrita]] (ants, bees and wasps) from the rest of order [[Hymenoptera]] (i.e., from the [[paraphyletic]] suborder [[Symphyta]]).

